findings.problem:
§3 defines a grammar constant as a name containing «» or any constant of a Becoming.Grammar* module, and a grammar law as any such theorem. That admits, as grammar laws:
- GrammarLaws' list and number helpers;
- Lean-generated auxiliaries (`«及».brecOn`, `.sizeOf_spec`);
- witnesses about one constructed system (`«薪尽火传»`, `«两火以薪定理»`, which names TwoFlames and Flame; `«昼夜往来不穷»`);
- 47 theorems in GrammarCourse, GrammarRegularity, GrammarScope and GrammarCases whose types name model modules.

D1's grammar-only test does not exclude them. It passes helpers whose statements name nothing of the run, and it passes witnesses. So D1 does not capture "for every relation and class".

D3 lets a lemma use any of these theorems and still count as "from definitions alone".

The definition also diverges from:
- the measure's grammar test (is_grammar = '«' in name), on which the rule's "dependence: grammar" rests;
- master_laws.py's docstring, which says the number and list lemmas of GrammarLaws do not count;
- U4's corrected walk.

